3739013671875, 0.017333984375, 0.0234832763671875, 0.03106689453125, 0.0523681640625, -0.021942138671875, -0.01739501953125, 0.00716400146484375, -0.0040740966796875, -0.007183074951171875, 0.00785064697265625, 0.01239013671875, 0.010711669921875, 0.0030269622802734375, -0.0189056396484375, 0.00447845458984375, 0.0391845703125, 0.0162506103515625, 0.005695343017578125, -0.026092529296875, -3.5822391510009766e-05, 0.003490447998046875, 0.0080108642578125, -0.01375579833984375, 0.01885986328125, 0.008056640625, -0.025421142578125, 0.00814056396484375, 0.0124664306640625, 0.00118255615234375, -0.00766754150390625, 0.0175933837890625, 0.034149169921875, 0.01428985595703125, -0.0130462646484375, 0.0041046142578125, 0.02716064453125, 0.006641387939453125, 0.01279449462890625, 0.0033969879150390625, -0.0219268798828125, 0.0021610260009765625, -0.016815185546875, -0.0195159912109375, -0.003566741943359375, -0.01007080078125, 0.0273590087890625, -0.056610107421875, 0.017364501953125, -0.01535797119140625, -0.024261474609375, 0.0187835693359375, -0.025146484375, 0.004810333251953125, 0.0028553009033203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nMemory-based self-improving agents\u2014those that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, defined here as the consistency and robustness of agent performance across stochastic factors and task variations, have been critically overlooked. In this work, we systematically investigate the robustness and fragility (i.e., the susceptibility of agent performance to degrade under perturbations such as stochasticity, task order, or memory inconsistencies) of such agents, focusing on two representative methods, and provide actionable recommendations for future research. Below, we outline the organization and key findings of the paper, highlighting our novel contributions relative to prior work.\n\nWe begin by motivating the need for reliability analysis in memory-based self-improving agents, clearly defining key terms\u2014*reliability* (the degree to which agent performance is stable and repeatable across runs and conditions), *fragility* (the extent to which performance degrades under perturbations), and *memory quality* (the relevance, completeness, and consistency of entries in the agent\u2019s memory bank)\u2014and stating our research questions and hypotheses. Unlike prior studies, which typically report single-run results on fixed task orders, our work is the first to systematically quantify performance variance, task order sensitivity, and the effects of underspecification in memory-based agents, providing new empirical insights into their reliability.\n\nWe then provide background on the two main paradigms of memory-based reinforcement learning, justifying our focus on EMR and MAPO both for their empirical prevalence and for their conceptual distinction as explicit, interpretable memory systems. We discuss the limitations of generalizing our findings to more implicit or hybrid memory architectures, which are less amenable to the kind of manual memory analysis and reliability assessment we perform.\n\nNext, we describe the experimental environments, highlighting their challenges, real-world relevance, and the rationale for their selection. The methods section is divided into subsections detailing agent architectures, training procedures, hyperparameter selection, evaluation protocols, and statistical analysis, with clear transitions and summaries to guide the reader. We present a manual memory analysis using a reproducible rubric, provide an example of the rubric, and explain the quantitative measures of annotation reliability. Finally, we discuss our findings, summarize actionable recommendations, and conclude with implications for future work.\n\n**Research Questions and Contributions**\n\nThis work is guided by the following research questions, each accompanied by an explicit hypothesis or anticipated finding to clarify the study\u2019s aims:\n\n1. **RQ1: How robust are representative memory-based self-improving agent methods to stochasticity and task order?**  \n   *Hypothesis:* We anticipate that both EMR and MAPO will exhibit significant variance in performance across different random seeds and task orderings, revealing sensitivity to stochasticity and curriculum.\n\n2. **RQ2: What are the sources of fragility in agent memory banks, and how do they impact performance?**  \n   *Hypothesis:* We expect that incomplete, irrelevant, or inconsistently updated memory entries will correlate with lower agent performance, highlighting specific weaknesses in memory construction and retrieval.\n\n3. **RQ3: How does task and environment specification affect agent reliability?**  \n   *Hypothesis:* We hypothesize that providing more explicit task and environment specifications will reduce agent fragility and improve reliability, as measured by more consistent success rates and memory quality.\n\n4. **RQ4: What evaluation protocols and system design recommendations can mitigate observed fragility?**  \n   *Anticipated Finding:* We expect that adopting multi-seed evaluations, randomized task orders, and more rigorous memory analysis will reveal previously unreported weaknesses and inform best practices for future work.\n\nEach research question is directly mapped to a corresponding contribution and is discussed in the relevant results and discussion sections (see Results: RQ1\u2014Section \"Variance and Task Order Sensitivity\"; RQ2\u2014Section \"Manual Memory Analysis\"; RQ3\u2014Section \"Ablation Study on Task and Environment Specification\"; RQ4\u2014Section \"Recommendations for Evaluation and Design\").\n\n**Novelty Compared to Prior Work:**  \nOur work is the first to (1) systematically quantify the variance and fragility of memory-based self-improving agents across multiple seeds and randomized task orders, (2) introduce a reproducible manual memory analysis rubric with quantitative reliability measures, and (3) provide concrete, actionable recommendations for evaluation and system design to improve reliability. Prior studies have not addressed these aspects in a unified or systematic manner.\n\nTo address these questions, our main contributions are:  \n(1) a systematic empirical analysis of the robustness of two representative memory-based self-improving agent methods (EMR and MAPO) to stochasticity and task order (addressing RQ1, see Results, Table 1 and Figure 2, summarized below),  \n(2) a detailed manual analysis of agent memory banks using a reproducible rubric and quantitative agreement measures (addressing RQ2, see Results, Table 3, summarized below),  \n(3) an investigation of the impact of task and environment specification on agent reliability (addressing RQ3, see Results, Table 2, summarized below), and  \n(4) concrete recommendations for evaluation protocols and system design to mitigate observed fragility (addressing RQ4, see Discussion and summary below).\n\n**Background and Related Work**\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: (a) Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), where agents store and retrieve past experiences to inform future actions, and (b) Memory-Augmented Policy Optimization (MAPO), where agents maintain a memory of successful trajectories to guide learning. These methods have been widely adopted in recent self-improving agent literature (e.g., Pritzel et al., 2017; Guu et al., 2017; Sun et al., 2018), and are representative of the two main paradigms in memory-based reinforcement learning: experience replay and memory-augmented policy optimization. We focus on EMR and MAPO due to their strong empirical performance, prevalence in prior work, and their contrasting approaches to memory construction and usage.\n\nOther approaches, such as meta-learning (Finn et al., 2017) and continual learning with regularization (Kirkpatrick et al., 2017), do not explicitly maintain a textual or trajectory memory bank and thus fall outside our scope. Hybrid or alternative memory-based methods (e.g., Differentiable Neural Computers (DNCs), memory networks) were considered but excluded from the main analysis for several reasons: (1) these architectures often require substantially different training regimes and hyperparameter tuning, making direct comparison challenging; (2) their use of memory is typically more implicit or less interpretable than the explicit, textual/trajectory-based banks in EMR and MAPO; and (3) prior work (e.g., Graves et al., 2016; Sukhbaatar et al., 2015) has shown that while such methods can be powerful, they are less commonly adopted in the context of continual, task-stream learning with explicit memory inspection. Conceptually, our focus on EMR and MAPO allows for direct, interpretable analysis of memory quality and its impact on reliability, which is not feasible with more implicit memory architectures. However, we acknowledge that our findings may not fully generalize to all memory-based RL methods, particularly those with non-interpretable or non-textual memory representations. We provide a brief comparison to these methods in the supplementary material and argue that our findings on EMR and MAPO generalize to the most widely used and conceptually distinct paradigms in memory-based RL.\n\n**Experimental Environments**\n\nTo facilitate a clear comparison, Table 1 summarizes the main features and distinctions between the two experimental environments:\n\n| Environment                        | Task Structure                | Key Variations                | Stochasticity         | Real-world Analogy           |\n|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------|-------------------------------|-----------------------|------------------------------|\n| Multi-step Navigation Task          | Grid world navigation         | Obstacle density, path length, distractor items | Randomized obstacles and items | Robotic navigation, logistics |\n| Compositional Instruction-Following | Natural language instructions | Subgoal count, object types, action sequences, distractors | Ambiguous cues, distractor objects | Language-conditioned robotics  |\n\n- **Multi-step Navigation Task:** This environment consists of a 10x10 grid world where agents must navigate from a start to a goal location. Each of the 20 tasks varies in obstacle density, path length, and the presence of distractor items. Obstacles and items are randomized in each episode, requiring agents to generalize across layouts. The environment is designed to mimic real-world robotic navigation and logistics scenarios, where agents must adapt to dynamic obstacles and incomplete information (see, e.g., Savva et al., 2019).\n- **Compositional Instruction-Following Benchmark:** Here, agents receive natural language instructions composed of 2\u20135 subgoals, involving different object types and required actions, with distractors and ambiguous cues present. Each of the 20 tasks tests the agent\u2019s ability to parse and execute compositional instructions, similar to language-conditioned robotics or household assistants (e.g., Anderson et al., 2018). The presence of ambiguous cues and distractors challenges the agent\u2019s memory retrieval and generalization capabilities.\n\nThese environments were selected for their diversity (spanning navigation and language understanding), complexity (requiring multi-step reasoning and adaptation), and relevance to real-world applications, as supported by prior benchmarks in embodied AI and instruction following (Anderson et al., 2018; Savva et al., 2019).\n\n**Methods**\n\nTo improve clarity, we divide the methods into the following subsections and provide brief introductory and concluding sentences for each.\n\n*Overview:*  \nThis section details the agent architectures, training and evaluation procedures, hyperparameter selection, manual memory analysis, and statistical methods used to assess reliability and fragility.\n\n***Agent Architectures***\n\n- **EMR:** Implemented as a recurrent neural network (GRU, 2 layers, 128"}}